{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_vrtx_011CfzRxTtgNrH6pyZt5DR8X", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I recovered all 11 records and wrote them to `/app/recovered.json`, sorted by id.\n\n**What was wrong:** the WAL file had been scrambled by XOR-ing every byte with `0x42`. I spotted this from the file header: a valid WAL starts with `37 7f 06 82`, but this one started with `75 3d 44 c0`, which is exactly that value XOR'd with `0x42`. The long runs of `0x42` (the letter `B`) were zero bytes scrambled the same way.\n\n**Fix:** I reversed the XOR on the whole WAL file. SQLite then read it normally and `PRAGMA integrity_check` returned `ok`.\n\n**Recovered data:**\n\n| id | name | value |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | apple | 150 |\n| 2 | banana | 250 |\n| 3 | cherry | 300 |\n| 4 | date | 400 |\n| 5 | elderberry | 500 |\n| 6 | fig | 600 |\n| 7 | grape | 700 |\n| 8 | honeydew | 800 |\n| 9 | kiwi | 900 |\n| 10 | lemon | 1000 |\n| 11 | mango | 1100 |\n\nThe WAL also changed existing rows: apple and banana now have the WAL's updated values, as well as rows 6–11 being added.\n\nWhen my reading connection closed, SQLite merged the WAL changes into `main.db` and deleted `main.db-wal`. So `main.db` on its own now holds all 11 rows. Copies of the original files are inside the container at `/tmp/main.db.bak` and `/tmp/wal.enc.bak`."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 385, "cache_read_input_tokens": 18543, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 385, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0}, "output_tokens": 2}, "input_transformations": [], "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "1d15609d-2d2f-45b5-91f3-663a9db79c6c", "uuid": "8e7b0c54-a037-4f01-bf32-3419b45ffbcf", "timestamp": "2026-10-13T14:56:39.645Z", "request_id": "req_vrtx_011CfzRxTsCb3x8XmZJKzvFt"}
